# Clicking the board when it is an engine's turn starts the game.
#
# It did for years — boardClick() called model.start() + run() unconditionally —
# and then stopped, twice over and unremarked. 4c5dcfc moved start() behind "a
# stone is actually being placed", correctly, because merely picking a stone out
# of the bowl was flipping the phase to Playing on an empty board; the
# bot-to-move case went with it. 2d6b222 then refused the click outright, since
# `a.play` requires the turn to be the human's.
#
# It is back by asking availableActions() a *different* question — `a.start`,
# which is true exactly when the Start button lights — and dispatching the
# `start` command, so there is no second path into model.start() and run().

new_game 9
wait_idle
switch_player black mock
switch_player white human
wait_idle

# Black is the engine and it is Black's turn, so the game is startable and a
# stone cannot be placed by hand.
expect phase setup
expect can_start true
expect can_play false
# `running` is the game *loop*, which ADR-0008 starts at new_game so the engines
# can sync on an empty board — it is not "the match is under way". The phase is.

# The click that used to do nothing.
click 4 4
wait_until move_count >= 1
wait_idle

expect phase playing
expect black_stones 1
# ...and the point clicked is irrelevant: the engine chose the move, this was a
# Start. The engine plays 4,4 itself only by coincidence, so assert the turn
# passed rather than where the stone landed.
expect color_to_move W

# And it really was Start, not a stone placed by hand: nothing is in hand.
expect holds_stone false

# The other half of the rule — with a human to move the click still places a
# stone, in two clicks, and does not dispatch start.
click 6 6
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 6 6
wait_until move_count >= 2
wait_idle
expect white_stones 1
